# 🚀 DVC (Data Version Control) Tutorial

DVC acts just like Git, but for your large data files. Instead of storing massive CSVs in GitHub, DVC stores them in a cloud storage provider (like Google Drive, AWS S3, or a local network drive) and creates a tiny `.dvc` text file. You commit that tiny text file to Git instead.

Here is a step-by-step guide on how to set up DVC for your **Clinical-QA-NLP** project.

---

## Part 1: Setting up DVC (You do this once)

### 1. Install DVC
First, you need to install DVC in your Python virtual environment. Depending on where you want to store your data (e.g., Google Drive, S3, Azure), you install a specific plugin. 

For Google Drive:
```powershell
pip install "dvc[gdrive]"
```
*(If you want to use S3, use `pip install "dvc[s3]"`, etc.)*

### 2. Initialize DVC
Navigate to the root of your Git repository and initialize DVC. This creates a `.dvc/` hidden folder.
```powershell
cd C:\Users\84913\source\repos\SL\Clinical_QA_System\Clinical-QA-NLP
dvc init
git commit -m "Initialize DVC"
```

### 3. Configure Remote Storage
Tell DVC where to actually upload the heavy data files. For example, if you create a folder in Google Drive, you can grab its folder ID from the URL (e.g., `https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/<YOUR_FOLDER_ID>`).
```powershell
dvc remote add -d myremote gdrive://<YOUR_FOLDER_ID>
git add .dvc/config
git commit -m "Configure DVC remote storage"
```

---

## Part 2: Tracking Your Data

Now that DVC is set up, let's track the `backend/AI/data` folder you just created.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> When you use `dvc add`, DVC will automatically add the target folder to your `.gitignore` so you don't accidentally commit the heavy files to Git.

### 1. Add Data to DVC
```powershell
dvc add backend/AI/data
```
This command does two things:
1. It adds `backend/AI/data` to `.gitignore`.
2. It generates a tiny tracking file called `backend/AI/data.dvc`.

### 2. Commit the tracking file to Git
Commit the tiny `.dvc` file to Git. This tells your team "Here is the exact version of the data I am currently using."
```powershell
git add backend/AI/data.dvc backend/AI/.gitignore
git commit -m "Track AI dataset using DVC"
```

### 3. Push the data to your cloud storage
Upload the actual heavy data files to your Google Drive (or S3).
```powershell
dvc push
```

---

## Part 3: Collaborator Workflow

When a collaborator labels more data and wants to send it to you, this is the exact flow they follow:

### How a collaborator sends you updated data:
1. They add their newly labeled CSVs to the `backend/AI/data/raw/` folder on their computer.
2. They tell DVC to track the updates:
   ```powershell
   dvc add backend/AI/data
   ```
3. They commit the updated `.dvc` tracking file to GitHub:
   ```powershell
   git add backend/AI/data.dvc
   git commit -m "Add newly labeled data batch"
   git push origin main
   ```
4. They push the actual heavy files to cloud storage:
   ```powershell
   dvc push
   ```

### How YOU download their updated data:
When you want to train on their freshly labeled data, you just do this:

1. Pull the newest pointer files from GitHub:
   ```powershell
   git pull origin main
   ```
2. Tell DVC to download the actual data files that match the new pointers:
   ```powershell
   dvc pull
   ```

And just like that, your `backend/AI/data` folder will instantly update to contain all their newest labeled data!
